<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<section xml:id="chapter12_text-files-and-stream-states">
    <title>Text Files and Stream States</title>

    <p>
        Text-file programs commonly open a file, perform input or output, test
        whether each operation succeeded, and close the file. File stream objects
        can close themselves when they leave scope, but checking the stream after
        opening is still essential.
    </p>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter12_write-text-file">
        <title>Writing a text file</title>
        <program xml:id="program_chapter12_write-text-file"
                 language="cpp" line-numbers="yes">
            <code><![CDATA[
#include <fstream>

int main() {
    std::ofstream output_file{"scores.txt"};

    if (!output_file) {
        return 1;
    }

    output_file << "Amina,92\n";
    output_file << "Marco,85\n";
    return 0;
}
            ]]></code>
        </program>
        <p>
            Opening an <c>std::ofstream</c> normally creates a missing file or
            replaces existing contents. The file mode determines whether the
            program overwrites or appends data; that choice is discussed in
            Section 12.4.
        </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter12_read-text-file">
        <title>Reading tokens and lines</title>
        <program xml:id="program_chapter12_read-text-file"
                 language="cpp" line-numbers="yes">
            <code><![CDATA[
#include <fstream>
#include <iostream>
#include <string>

int main() {
    std::ifstream input_file{"scores.txt"};

    if (!input_file) {
        return 1;
    }

    std::string line;
    while (std::getline(input_file, line)) {
        std::cout << "Read line: " << line << '\n';
    }

    return 0;
}
            ]]></code>
        </program>
        <p>
            Formatted extraction such as <c>input_file &gt;&gt; score</c> is useful
            for whitespace-separated values. <c>std::getline()</c> is useful when
            the program must preserve the spaces and delimiters in a complete
            line.
        </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter12_stream-state-flags">
        <title>Stream-state flags</title>
        <table xml:id="table_chapter12_stream-states">
            <title>Common stream states</title>
            <tabular top="major" left="major" bottom="major" right="major">
            <col width="25%"/><col width="75%"/>
                <row header="yes">
                    <cell>State</cell>
                    <cell>Meaning</cell>
                </row>
                <row><cell><c>good()</c></cell><cell>No failure has been detected.</cell></row>
                <row><cell><c>eof()</c></cell><cell>The end of the input sequence was reached.</cell></row>
                <row><cell><c>fail()</c></cell><cell>A formatted operation failed or another recoverable failure occurred.</cell></row>
                <row><cell><c>bad()</c></cell><cell>A serious input/output failure occurred.</cell></row>
            </tabular>
        </table>
        <p>
            A read loop should normally use the input operation itself as its
            condition. The body then runs only after a complete requested value
            has been obtained.
        </p>
    </subsection>

    <exercise label="chapter12_2_line-input">
        <statement><p>Why is <c>std::getline()</c> useful for a record containing spaces?</p></statement>
        <choices randomize="yes">
            <choice><statement><p>It converts every line into a binary object.</p></statement><feedback><p><c>getline()</c> reads characters into a string.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice correct="yes"><statement><p>It reads through the line ending instead of stopping at the next whitespace token.</p></statement><feedback><p>This preserves spaces within the line.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p>It guarantees that the file exists.</p></statement><feedback><p>Opening and checking the file are separate operations.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p>It automatically validates the record fields.</p></statement><feedback><p>Parsing and validation still belong to the program.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p>It reads only the first line.</p></statement><feedback><p>A loop can call it repeatedly.</p></feedback></choice>
        </choices>
    </exercise>

    <exercise label="chapter12_2_read-loop">
        <statement><p>Select all correct reasons to test a file stream.</p></statement>
        <choices randomize="yes" multiple-correct="yes">
            <choice correct="yes"><statement><p>The file may not exist or may be inaccessible.</p></statement><feedback><p>Opening can fail before input begins.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice correct="yes"><statement><p>A read operation may reach end-of-file or encounter malformed input.</p></statement><feedback><p>Each operation can change the stream state.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p>Testing a stream causes all files to become binary.</p></statement><feedback><p>Stream tests do not change the file format.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p>A successful open guarantees every future read succeeds.</p></statement><feedback><p>Later input can still fail.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p><c>eof()</c> should always be checked before attempting a read.</p></statement><feedback><p>The read operation should usually be part of the loop condition.</p></feedback></choice>
        </choices>
    </exercise>

    <exercise label="chapter12_2_text-code" number="yes" adaptive="yes" indentation="show">
        <statement><p>Arrange the blocks to open a text file and print each complete line.</p></statement>
        <blocks>
            <block order="1"><cline>std::ifstream input_file{"notes.txt"};</cline></block>
            <block order="2"><cline>if (!input_file) { return 1; }</cline></block>
            <block order="3"><cline>std::string line;</cline></block>
            <block order="4"><cline>while (std::getline(input_file, line)) {</cline></block>
            <block order="5"><cline>    std::cout &lt;&lt; line &lt;&lt; '\n';</cline></block>
            <block order="6"><cline>}</cline></block>
        </blocks>
    </exercise>
</section>
